<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Quiberon Bay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Quiberon Bay · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Quiberon Bay was a naval battle fought in November 1759 off the coast of Brittany during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. The British fleet under Admiral Hawke defeated the French fleet, ending French plans to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The defeat crippled French naval power and contributed to the losses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay.jpg" alt="Battle of Quiberon Bay" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Paton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Quiberon_Bay,_20_November_1759_RMG_BHC0397.tiff" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle, called the Bataille des Cardinaux by the French, was fought on 20 and 21 November 1759 in Quiberon Bay, near Saint-Nazaire. By 1759 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was spending more than 60% of its revenue on its debt, and its navy was overstretched and more than 9,000 men short of full crews. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>'s ministers planned a direct invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>: an army of 17,000 gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a> and nearly 100 transports near Quiberon Bay, to be escorted by the fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a>.</p><p>In August the British under Edward Boscawen broke the French Mediterranean Fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lagos-1759">Battle of Lagos</a>, but Choiseul still contemplated a landing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. When a westerly gale in early November drove Sir Edward Hawke's blockading fleet back to Torbay, Hubert de Brienne slipped out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> on the 14th to collect the transports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 20 November de Brienne chased Robert Duff's small squadron off Belle-Isle and was still scattered when Hawke's 24 ships of the line came into sight. In a very violent wind and high seas he made for the shelter of the bay, behind its maze of shoals and reefs. Hawke signalled a general chase and followed under full sail. As the French rounded Les Cardinaux, the rocks at the end of the Quiberon peninsula, the British caught their rear.</p><p>Formidable surrendered just before 4 pm. Thésée capsized with only 22 survivors when water poured through her open lower gunports, Superbe also capsized, and Héros struck her flag and ran aground. With darkness falling Hawke signalled his fleet to anchor. During the night eight French ships escaped to Rochefort and others jettisoned their guns to cross the bar of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilaine">Vilaine</a>. De Brienne's flagship Soleil Royal was wrecked on the Four Shoal and burned. The French lost 2,520 men killed, the British about 300 killed or wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The power of the French fleet was broken for the rest of the war, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> the invasion threat was removed. Alfred Mahan called the battle the Trafalgar of this war: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s fleets were free to act against the French colonies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> could not reinforce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> after Sainte-Foy the next spring. France defaulted on its debt. The battle was part of Britain's annus mirabilis of 1759.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Quiberon Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The loss of naval supremacy to Britain, confirmed at the Battle of Lagos and at <b>Quiberon Bay</b> in 1759, was the proximate military cause of the first empire's collapse.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) brought Robert Clive’s victory at the Battle of Plassey in 1757, the conquest of Quebec after James Wolfe’s victory on the Plains of Abraham in 1759 and naval victories at Lagos and <b>Quiberon Bay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In a gale off Brittany, Admiral Edward Hawke pursued the French fleet among the rocks and shoals of <b>Quiberon Bay</b> and destroyed or scattered it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-20">20 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Decimus Brutus defeated them in a calm, probably in <b>Quiberon Bay</b>, by cutting their rigging with hooked poles.</blockquote><a href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a></li></ul></section>
